The book is 1001/1003 with no resting orders. My earlier buy and sell at 1000 crossed with each other, which was an unintended self-trade and artificial turnover. I'm avoiding that now by quoting on separate price levels: a bid at 1001 (joining the best bid) and an ask at 1003 (joining the best ask). The bid sits below my ask, so the two quotes cannot cross. Size is small (5 lots) given the flat inventory.

```json
{
  "actions": [
    {"type": "limit", "side": "buy", "qty": 5, "price": 1001},
    {"type": "limit", "side": "sell", "qty": 5, "price": 1003}
  ],
  "explanation": "Post small two-sided quotes at the touch (bid 1001, ask 1003) with non-crossing prices to provide genuine liquidity and avoid self-trading; inventory is flat and well within limits."
}
```

The history shows my previous quotes were placed at the same price on both sides, so the sell crossed my own resting buy and filled against it. That is self-trading and produces no genuine liquidity or spread. I will avoid that and quote at different prices.

Current book: best bid 1003, best ask 1005, a 2-wide spread. There are no open orders and inventory is 0. A buy at 1004 and a sell at 1005 would be non-marketable and cannot cross each other, but a sell at 1005 would only join the existing ask queue. A better way to improve both sides is to post a buy at 1004 and a sell at 1006, or to sit at 1004 and 1005 for tighter quoting. I'll quote a buy at 1004 (inside the spread, improving the bid) and a sell at 1005 (joining the best ask) in small size. Both rest without crossing the book or each other.

